        <h1 style="font-size:32px; color:#121481; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:30px;">
            Brigada Eskwela Anti-Dengue Campaign: Ensuring a Safe and Healthy Learning Environment
        </h1>

        <!-- Centered Image -->
        <img src="/Images/blog2.jpg" alt="Brigada Eskwela Anti-Dengue" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; border-radius:10px; object-fit:cover; margin-bottom:20px;" />

        <!-- Intro Paragraph -->
        <p style="font-size:18px; line-height:1.6; text-align:justify; margin-bottom:40px;">
            As the rainy season approaches, schools across the country brace themselves not only for a fresh start but also for the rise of potential health threats such as dengue fever. In response to this growing concern, the Department of Education (DepEd) continues to promote its Brigada Eskwela Anti-Dengue Campaign, which plays a vital role in ensuring that schools remain safe and healthy learning environments for students. As the rainy season approaches, schools across the country brace themselves not only for a fresh start but also for the rise of potential health threats such as dengue fever. In response to this growing concern, the Department of Education (DepEd) continues to promote its Brigada Eskwela Anti-Dengue Campaign, which plays a vital role in ensuring that schools remain safe and healthy learning environments for students.
        </p>

        <!-- Main Content Sections -->
        <h2 style="font-size:28px; color:#121481; margin-top:40px; margin-bottom:20px; text-align:left;">The Growing Threat of Dengue</h2>
        <p style="font-size:18px; line-height:1.6; margin-bottom:20px; text-align:justify;">
            Dengue is a mosquito-borne viral infection that spreads through the bite of an infected Aedes aegypti mosquito. With symptoms ranging from high fever and joint pain to more severe complications like hemorrhagic fever, dengue is a serious threat, especially during the rainy season when stagnant water becomes a breeding ground for mosquitoes.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size:18px; line-height:1.6; margin-bottom:20px; text-align:justify;">
            Schools are particularly vulnerable, as many have outdoor spaces where water can accumulate, including playgrounds, gardens, and uncovered areas. Left unchecked, these areas can quickly become mosquito breeding sites, putting the health of students and staff at risk.
        </p>

        <h2 style="font-size:28px; color:#121481; margin-top:40px; margin-bottom:20px; text-align:left;">Key Components of the Brigada Eskwela Anti-Dengue Campaign</h2>
        <p style="font-size:18px; line-height:1.6; margin-bottom:20px; text-align:justify;">
            The Anti-Dengue Campaign under Brigada Eskwela aims to minimize the risk of dengue outbreaks in schools by focusing on preventive measures and educating the community. Here are the key components of the campaign:
        </p>

        <h3>Regular Clean-Up Drives</h3>
        <p style="font-size:18px; line-height:1.6; margin-bottom:20px; text-align:justify;">
            One of the main focuses of the campaign is the “Search and Destroy” method, where volunteers are encouraged to eliminate potential mosquito breeding grounds. This includes cleaning up clogged drainage systems, removing stagnant water in flower pots and tires, and ensuring that trash is disposed of properly. Regular clean-up drives are organized to maintain cleanliness and reduce the risk of mosquito proliferation.
        </p>

        <h3>Information Dissemination</h3>
        <p style="font-size:18px; line-height:1.6; margin-bottom:20px; text-align:justify;">
            Educating the school community about the dangers of dengue and how to prevent it is essential. Information sessions, posters, and flyers are distributed to raise awareness among students, parents, and school staff. Topics such as the importance of wearing protective clothing, using insect repellent, and recognizing the symptoms of dengue are covered during these campaigns.
        </p>

        <h3>Installation of Mosquito Nets and Screens</h3>
        <p style="font-size:18px; line-height:1.6; margin-bottom:20px; text-align:justify;">
            Many schools install mosquito nets or screens in classrooms, windows, and doors to prevent mosquitoes from entering school buildings. This simple yet effective measure ensures that students can learn in a safer environment, reducing their exposure to dengue-carrying mosquitoes.
        </p>

        <h3>Larvicide Application</h3>
        <p style="font-size:18px; line-height:1.6; margin-bottom:20px; text-align:justify;">
            In some cases, schools work with local government units (LGUs) to apply safe, environment-friendly larvicides in water sources that cannot be easily drained, such as large water containers or ponds. This treatment helps eliminate mosquito larvae before they can develop into adult mosquitoes.
        </p>

        <h3>Health Monitoring and Immediate Response</h3>
        <p style="font-size:18px; line-height:1.6; margin-bottom:20px; text-align:justify;">
            Schools are encouraged to establish health monitoring systems to identify students who may be exhibiting symptoms of dengue. This allows for early detection and immediate response, including referral to local health centers or hospitals for proper diagnosis and treatment.
        </p>

        <h2 style="font-size:28px; color:#121481; margin-top:40px; margin-bottom:20px; text-align:left;">The Impact of the Anti-Dengue Campaign</h2>
        <p style="font-size:18px; line-height:1.6; margin-bottom:20px; text-align:justify;">
            The Brigada Eskwela Anti-Dengue Campaign has proven to be a vital tool in reducing the spread of dengue in schools and communities. By creating safer, cleaner school environments, students are less likely to be exposed to dengue-carrying mosquitoes. Additionally, the campaign fosters a sense of responsibility and unity among parents, teachers, and the broader community, showing that health and education go hand in hand.
        </p>

